===persian===

وقتی تو می‌خوانی
کسی نمی‌پرسد
برای چه می‌خوانی
برای که می‌خوانی.
وقتی پرنده‌ای می‌خواند
همه می‌دانند
برای چه می‌خواند
برای که می‌خواند.
وقتی من نقاشی می‌کنم
تو می‌پرسی
برای چه؟...
برای که؟...
پاییز ۱۳۶۸

===machine===

When you sing
no one asks
why you are singing
for whom you are singing.
When a bird sings
everyone knows
why it is singing
for whom it is singing.
When I paint
you ask
Why?...
For whom?...
Autumn 1368

===lantern===

When you sing
nobody asks
what you are singing for,
who you are singing for.
When a bird sings
everybody knows
what it is singing for,
who it is singing for.
When I paint
you ask
What for?...
For whom?...

Autumn 1368

===translation===

===footnotes===

Dedicated to Mohammad-Reza Shajarian (1940–2020), the great voice of Persian
classical song — which is the ground the joke stands on: nobody asks Shajarian to explain why
he sings. برای چه ('what for') and برای که ('for whom') differ by a single letter, so in Persian
the poem's last two lines are almost the same sound asked twice.

